              text: 'Sports for All U N I T This unit emphasises the importance of making sports accessible and inclusive for everyone, regardless of age, gender, varied ability, or socio-economic background. It highlights the concept of sports as a fundamental human right, as outlined in global frameworks like the UNESCO Charter of Physical Education and the European Sports Charter. This unit explores how sports can promote happiness, unity, and good health, while fostering social inclusion and breaking barriers. It also highlights the challenges faced by marginalised groups, such as a lack of access to facilities, financial constraints, societal attitudes, and provides measures to promote inclusiveness. These include developing accessible infrastructure, enforcing anti-discrimination policies, promoting grassroots programmes, and supporting traditional and indigenous games like Kabaddi and Kho-Kho. Through activities and discussions, students are encouraged to design inclusive games, explore barriers to participation, and propose solutions to make sports more accessible. The unit emphasises the transformative power of sports in building teamwork, self-esteem, and community bonds, while fostering peace, equality, and mutual respect. It inspires students to advocate for inclusiveness and work towards creating a society where sports are truly for all. Unit 4.indd 11-04-2026 03:41:24 Sport has the power to change the world. It has the power to inspire. It has the power to unite people in a way that little else does. — Nelson Mandela During a school’s annual sports day, the final race was about to begin. The crowd cheered as the whistle blew, but halfway down the track, one boy stumbled and fell. The other runners could have surged ahead—glory was just metres away. But something remarkable happened. One by one, they stopped.',
