              heading: 'Part 14',
              text: 'Now that we’ve thought about Rohan\'s situation, let’s understand the meaning of etiquette and how it applies to everyone. Etiquette means good manners and the right way of behaving with others. It helps us to be polite, respectful, and friendly in our daily life. General etiquette includes simple things like saying “thank you”, listening carefully when someone talks, and respecting other people’s space. Disability Etiquettes: Respecting Everyone with Dignity • If you were in Rohan’s place, what would you do? • How can we be helpful without making someone feel uncomfortable or different? • What does this story teach us about respecting people with disabilities? LET US REFLECT Unit 4.indd 11-04-2026 03:41:32 Disability Etiquettes: Respecting Everyone with Dignity Disability etiquettes are about the correct ways of behaving with people who have disabilities. It teaches us to treat them with respect and kindness, without pity or overprotecting them. People with disabilities want to be seen as individuals first, not just for their disability. By following disability etiquette, we can make them feel included, confident, and equal in society. What are General Etiquettes? These are basic principles of respectful, courteous behaviour toward all individuals: 1. Be Respectful: Treat everyone with dignity and consideration. 2. Be Inclusive: Don’t assume people can or can’t do things based on appearance. 3. Ask Before Helping: Never assume someone needs help— always ask first. 4. Use First-Person (when preferred): One example would be “person with a disability” instead of “disabled person” (though some prefer identity-first, like “autistic person”—so follow individual preferences). 5. Don’t Stare, Point, or Make Jokes: Avoid drawing attention to someone’s disability or differences.',
