              text: 'Unit 4.indd 11-04-2026 03:41:34 Khel Praveen Low Energy Availability This happens when athletes do not eat enough food to balance the energy they spend during exercise. • It may occur due to dieting, fear of weight gain, or eating disorders such as: – Anorexia Nervosa: An extreme fear of gaining weight, leading to eating very little or over-exercising. – Bulimia Nervosa: Eating large amounts of food and then using unhealthy methods to remove calories, such as vomiting or using laxatives. • Common symptoms include fatigue, weight loss, stress fractures, irregular periods, anxiety, and weakness. Proper nutrition and professional guidance can prevent or correct this condition. Low Bone Mineral Density (BMD) Bone health depends on proper nutrition, hormones, and exercise. When women have low oestrogen levels or poor diet, bones become fragile and weak, a condition known as osteoporosis. • This can cause stress fractures, pain, or deformity later in life. • Bone growth and strengthening happen mainly during childhood and adolescence, so maintaining proper diet and physical activity during these years is vital. Sports are not just about winning medals or breaking records. For women, they represent freedom, empowerment, and the opportunity to challenge stereotypes. Every girl who picks up a cricket bat, laces up running shoes, or steps onto a basketball court is making a statement about her capabilities and her right to participate fully in society. Myths and Taboos A common myth is that girls should not exercise during their period. But in truth, light exercise often helps reduce cramps and improve mood. Even Olympic champions compete during menstruation. What matters is comfort, hygiene, and listening to your own body.',
