              text: 'Activity Talk to your grandparents, neighbours, or any older person you know and learn about the games or sports they used to play when they were young. Find out: 1. Which games or sports did they enjoy most? 2. Why did they stop playing regularly? 3. Do they still play or sta y active in some way today? 4. What did sports teach them about life? Present their sporting journey to your class and discuss what you learned about sports, ageing, and staying active throughout life. Unit 4.indd 11-04-2026 03:41:35 Khel Praveen Activity Learn a game that your grandparents, neighbours, or elders used to play when they were young. Ask them to explain the rules, equipment, and how it is played. Teach this game to your classmates and play it together. Then discuss amongst yourselves, 1. What did you enjoy about the game? 2. How is it different from the games you usually play? 3. What skills does it develop? Exercises 1. List five benefits of regular physical activity for older adults. 2. How does exercise help in preventing heart disease and diabetes? 3. Exercise is called “the best medicine for ageing”. Justify your answer. Answer in brief 1. Suggest three ways your school can promote inclusiveness in sports. 2. Design one inclusive game and explain how it ensures teamwork and fairness. 3. How do sports help persons with disabilities in building their confidence and independence? 4. What barriers commonly prevent persons with disabilities from participating in sports? 5. What changes in “Equipment” can help students with visual impairments participate in games? 6. How can pairing students (People component of STEP) encourage teamwork and empathy? 7.',
