              heading: 'Part 1',
              text: 'Sports U N I T This unit introduces students to the wide and exciting world of sports and physical activities, highlighting their importance in maintaining a healthy and active lifestyle. It begins by focusing on India’s rich heritage of indigenous martial arts such as Thang-Ta, which combines body movements, breathing techniques, and weapon skills to develop physical control and mental balance. These traditional practices reflect cultural values and promote discipline and self-defence. The unit further explores various tr aditional Indian sport and martial arts like Mallakhamb, Gatka, and Kalaripayattu. These activities not only improve strength, flexibility, and coordination but also teach focus, courage, and respect. They serve as an important link between physical training and cultural traditions, helping students understand the significance of India’s historical sporting practices. In addition, the unit covers modern and international sports, including combat sports like Judo, Wrestling, Taekwondo, and Boxing, as well as popular games like Hockey, Basketball, and Cricket. It also introduces individual sports like Archery, Tennis, explaining their fundamental skills. Overall, this unit aims to develop awareness about the role of sports in building physical fitness, mental strength, teamwork, and sportsmanship. It encourages students to actively participate in different sports and appreciate their value in personal development, cultural understanding, and leading a balanced and healthy life. This unit commences with introduction to gymnastics and swimming which is considered as the foundation of multiple sports. Unit 5.indd 11-04-2026 03:42:06 G ymnastics The word gymnastics has been derived from the Greek word Gymnos meaning “naked art”. In ancient Greek, the word Gymnastics was used to denote the exercise done with the naked body.',
