              text: 'Gatka equipment used in demonstrations and competitions are stick and farri , chakra and chain, dhaal and kripaan , khanda and kataar , talwar and mace, teer and dang, etc. The concept of soul teaching in Gatka training reflects in our day-to-day living. Realising the importance of time, taking quick and right decisions at the time of real need, not intruding in anyone’s rights, not surrendering against evil deeds, living a simple and a principled life are qualities that come easily in a Gatkabaj. Dhala Buffalo- hide shield Jangi mojeh War shoes with toe blades Chair aina Iron armour plates Jangi kara war bracelets Sanjo Iron chainmail Toradar bandook matchlock rifle Trishool Mukh Trident in turban Kartar Dagger in waistband S W or DS an D Wea P on S Tegh 10-fist-long sword Unit 5.indd Indigenious Martial Arts and Sport k alar Ipayattu Kalari means a space where one gets training and Payattu means practising. References cited in the Vedas prove that Kalaripayattu has existed for 3,000 years, with deep roots in the social and cultural life of Keralam. Like yoga āsanas , Kalaripayattu is also inspired by the movements and stances of animals such as boar, lion, raptors, elephants, etc., and was primarily developed to counter wildlife. The British banned the practice, fearing it might make Indian citizens physically strong, emotionally balanced and mentally stable. Despite such restrictions, a few dedicated masters safeguarded this ancient art by practising it in isolation and secrecy, and it was resurrected after independence. With strong principles at its foundation, Kalaripayattu develops balance and flexibility of mind and body, emphasising speed, agility, co-ordination and discipline.',
